WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:12.569
We move now to item seven which is on the
organisational alignment and capability programme.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:18.090
I will ask Karen to speak to us but invite
Simon, if he wishes to, to say some words

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.000
by way of introduction.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:25.429
Simon Stevens: Thank you. We spoke about this
last time. Some of the changes that we are

00:00:25.429 --> 00:00:30.500
making here are changes that we would want
to make, one year into the organisation, in

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:35.960
order to adjust in light of the circumstances
we find ourselves in. Some of them are changes

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:41.149
that we are being required to make, in order
to reduce our running costs, in line with

00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:46.809
the broader redeployment of cash from back
office to front-line health services for 2015/16,

00:00:46.809 --> 00:00:53.809
as a contribution to the wider pressures that
the NHS is under. This is a piece of work

00:00:54.879 --> 00:01:01.699
that we will be consulting our staff formally
on, beginning on 1st October and Karen will

00:01:01.699 --> 00:01:04.750
update you on where things stand.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:11.750
Karen Wheeler: Thanks, Simon. To reiterate,
as we talked about last time, this programme

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:22.600
meets a combination of objectives. It is about
reviewing where we are in the organisation

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:33.040
and ensuring that it is geared up to meet
its current priorities and has the right capabilities.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:40.369
Secondly, it is to ensure that it is going
to be capable of doing the things that we

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:46.549
need to do going forward. In particular, all
of the work that we have been talking about

00:01:46.549 --> 00:01:52.380
today in terms of the forward plan is a key
area which is only just emerging. We shall

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:59.159
need to think about that. The third area is
the savings that we do not want to make but

00:01:59.159 --> 00:02:04.810
need to make to live within our baselines
for 2015/16.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:11.810
Clearly the need to make those savings for
2015/16 has probably driven us to try to make

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.720
these changes as quickly as possible so that
we could stabilise the workforce as quickly

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:25.040
as possible and ensure that the workforce
and management are able to continue to manage

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:31.829
delivery through winter and for the rest of
this year. That has driven a tight timescale

00:02:31.829 --> 00:02:35.730
for making the changes happen.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:42.730
We had hoped to start the consultation slightly
earlier, as we indicated in the paper, but

00:02:43.269 --> 00:02:50.269
have a little bit more to do for ourselves
to confirm that the structural changes we

00:02:50.739 --> 00:02:57.739
are making will properly meet our objectives,
are clear enough and ensure that we are capable

00:02:58.949 --> 00:03:05.949
of meeting both the organisation's current
and future needs. That is probably the key

00:03:07.479 --> 00:03:12.040
area that we are going to be spending some
time on over the next week as we finalise

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:17.699
these changes prior to the consultation being
launched on 1 October.

00:03:17.699 --> 00:03:23.630
We have already had discussions with the trade
unions about this and are very conscious of

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:29.850
the staff concerns. Clearly, one of the biggest
risks associated with this is, of course,

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:36.850
the impact on staff and the subsequent impact
on the continuing successful delivery into

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:45.090
both performance issues and all of the work
that we have been outlining and discussing

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:49.510
today. That is a key risk.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:56.510
A second key risk I wanted to flag is the
specific one around whether we have the right

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:03.019
resources in the right place for the future
and that is something that we will be discussing

00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:10.019
in more detail later and through the next
week. We are hoping to provide assurance to

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:16.639
all of ourselves and to you before we start
the formal consultation.

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:22.390
I think that is all I probably wanted to say.
The content is in the paper.

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:29.350
I would have hoped to have been able to provide
you with more detail about the actual numbers

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:36.350
at this stage but, again, that is some detail
that we are still refining. Our original estimates

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:47.000
of the numbers of staff who might have been
affected were something over 600 of posts

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:52.600
that might have been affected. It appears
that the numbers that we are working on will

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:59.480
be significantly less than that, but that
is what I am not able to confirm today, and

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:04.410
we will be tightening that up and confirming
it over the next week or so. That is all I

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:06.370
really wanted to say.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:12.060
Malcolm Grant: Karen, thank you very much
for that. Let invite observations from members

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:19.060
of the Board. I think I heard something sotto
voce from the Deputy Chairman, but I will

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:20.700
pass on to Ciaran.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:26.850
Ciaran Devane: I think we have always said
that 18 months on we would look at the organisation

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:33.640
because the chance of having designed the
right structure from a blank sheet was not

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:39.940
going to be high. I completely get that and
agree with it. You could even convince me

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:43.080
that 15% of our resources would not have been
in the right place.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:50.080
I am just grumpy about the slightly arbitrary
nature of what we have been asked and told

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:59.840
to do. Particularly from the point of view
of staff who had quite a difficult time being

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:06.650
reorganised two years ago and now there this
prospect of that again and the distraction

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:11.400
from the day job at a critical time, at exactly
the time we are going to planning. Just a

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:13.180
register of grumpiness, I am afraid, Chairman.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:13.990
Speaker: Hear, hear.

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:20.210
Malcolm Grant: I am not unaccustomed to it.
No, I think it is a very valid point and it

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:21.920
may be that Victor wishes to build on that.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:26.220
Victor Adebowale: I think it is a good point.
I think there is an impact of episodic change

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:30.940
on organisational culture, which is redolent
throughout the history of the NHS. People

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:35.400
get used to surviving it and that is, in itself,
a major risk.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:42.400
I might make another point, which is that
it strikes me that yesterday, when we had

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:50.430
the experience of the NHS Citizens, I am still
concerned about how that experience is reflected

00:06:53.380 --> 00:07:00.380
in the alignment of the organisation. I can
understand the structural form and the need

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:05.560
to do it. It is pragmatic. I get it. Do not
particularly like it. It is what it is. What

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:12.560
I am concerned about is the loss of an opportunity
to embed or to start the culture-values alignment

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:20.940
in keeping with our stated actions publicly.
That is the bit that really worries me, because

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:27.740
it could undermine everything that we have
said about how we want to operate.

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:34.740
Moira Gibb: I agree wholeheartedly with those
earlier comments. I just wanted to ask whether,

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:42.590
given what happened when we formed our own
diversity-averse staff, can we have particular

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:47.250
attention paid to the diversity impact of
these changes too?

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:54.250
Ed Smith: I have two points. Firstly, I suspect,
given the speed and the nature of what we

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:03.550
did when we did it, it is inconceivable that
we could have got everything right. I think

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:10.550
that part of what we are doing is putting
things right which, with the reflection of

00:08:11.389 --> 00:08:16.900
time and if given more time, we might have
done differently. I think it is quite important

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:23.900
to identify that and the benefits of knowledge
of operating the system differently.

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:33.690
The second point is that nobody likes to be
told what to do. We need to know if that is

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:40.690
what we are having to do: lots of organisations
are having to do it. This is not just about

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:51.210
doing everything we did before with a lot
less; it is about what we therefore have to

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:58.210
give up doing, having an honest conversation
that we simply cannot do X or Y anymore, because

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:05.220
without that conversation our accountability
is shot.

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:12.220
David Roberts: Malcolm, just to build on that
point from Ed. As we discussed at the audit

00:09:13.339 --> 00:09:20.339
committee, I think it is important that we
understand the nature in which financial controls

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:26.960
are being changed as a consequence of the
organisational realignment, and that we can

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:32.779
be satisfied that the new control environment
is robust and can be embedded quickly where

00:09:32.779 --> 00:09:35.310
things have changed.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:42.310
Malcolm Grant: All very valuable points, and
we will watch very closely. It is work in

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:43.890
progress.

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:50.890
Karen Wheeler: Shall I respond to some of
those points? I think it is really important

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:58.720
to give you as much assurance as we can on
all of those points. I think we were equally

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:06.400
grumpy about the numbers and, as Simon said,
would not have chosen to do this; we would

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:12.470
have chosen to make some changes to the organisation,
but not in terms of stripping out the level

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:18.440
of numbers and resources that we are doing.
It is causing some difficult decisions: there

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:23.850
is no question that every single directorate,
the National Directorate and the Field Force

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:30.850
are having to make some really difficult decisions.
That does have a consequence for us all.

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:41.779
One of the things that became very clear when
I joined the organisation was the general

00:10:41.779 --> 00:10:48.450
question by everybody, 'What is this organisation
for? What is it really about and what is its

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:55.450
core purpose?' In a sense everybody is having
to really focus on what is fundamentally important,

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:04.820
and using that to make the choices where those
choices are being made. It is not good, but

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:10.810
it is helping us to make some of those choices.
We need to be clear about that because there

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:16.130
are some things that we are going to have
to stop doing.

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:22.050
There are certainly also things that we are
still going to do but do less of: they are

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:28.089
quite important things and we need to ensure
that everybody understands those. For example,

00:11:28.089 --> 00:11:35.089
I know Barbara would say that the Field Force
is going to be reduced: there will be fewer

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:45.160
senior people across the patch in the Field
Force, covering wider geographies, and as

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:50.370
a result of that they simply will not be able
to go to every single Health and Wellbeing

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:56.020
Board and every single Safeguarding Board.
They will, though, prioritise those which

00:11:56.020 --> 00:12:02.950
are absolutely important in challenged, difficult
economies and circumstances, so we will be

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:06.330
more focused in how we work.

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:12.700
We are also trying to work together on how
we can work more efficiently and effectively

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:18.410
across our organisation. That was always one
of the key objectives of the stock take that

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:25.410
I did with Ros Roughton when I first came
in, that Simon asked me to do. Some of that

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:33.670
is about us joining up more effectively, working
across the organisation more efficiently,

00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:40.670
and therefore being able to avoid some duplication
and confusion in the way that we are working.

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:50.020
Those are the opportunities that we are able
to take, I think; it does not answer everything

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:52.920
that we need to do.

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:59.920
I wanted to come back to Victor's point because
it is absolutely important that we take the

00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:06.020
opportunity. We are naturally focusing right
now, because we need to, on the structural

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:13.020
changes which are putting posts and people
at risk. That is the primary focus right now

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:21.170
of this change programme. However, we have
done quite a lot of work on how we need to

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:28.170
operate more corporately as an organisation,
and we have more work to be done around that.

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:38.940
Although that does not specifically address
the points that you make, Victor, nut what

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:45.940
came out from all of yesterday and all of
our engagement with NHS Citizen is the need

00:13:47.899 --> 00:13:54.899
to build that closeness to the patient in
everything that we do, and also to change

00:13:56.760 --> 00:14:03.760
ourselves, our own culture in the way that
we operate, so that we can influence the culture

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:09.550
in the wider NHS, because I think that is
what came out so strongly yesterday. There

00:14:09.550 --> 00:14:14.890
is a lot more that we need to do - we have
not embedded that into plans yet - but I do

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:20.649
think that we still have the opportunity to
do that in the way that we implement the changes

00:14:20.649 --> 00:14:27.649
going forward. It's not a lost opportunity;
I think we need to take that opportunity still.

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:35.110
Finally, we did have a really useful session
with our Audit and Risk Committee on the risks

00:14:36.040 --> 00:14:42.390
and controls. I think we are all very conscious
that we are not in as strong a position as

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:46.730
we would like to be in any case on our control
environment. There is a lot more change that

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:52.610
needs to be done, and indeed some of the change
that we have specifically identified as part

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:57.600
of the stock take and as part of this piece
of work is to improve those controls. Some

00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:04.600
of those are to improve controls, but also
to streamline: to improve flexibility and

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:11.600
our ability to get our business done. Procurement
is a very good example of where we are looking

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:18.600
to streamline and not reduce control, but
certainly reduce the complexity and the bureaucracy

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:27.060
associated with the processes that we have
in place. I think by doing that we will improve

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:34.050
our controls, make it more efficient, and
give the business a better opportunity to

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:40.000
procure more effectively. That is one of the
work streams in our overall programme that

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:47.000
we are continuing to do, and there will be
a lot more work to be done along those lines.

00:15:48.310 --> 00:15:55.310
To provide a little more assurance around
the specific risk, one thing I think that

00:15:55.899 --> 00:16:02.800
is important is that while these changes are
quite significant in terms of the numbers

00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:09.800
of people affected, we are not fundamentally
changing the nature of our organisation's

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:17.050
structure. Every National Director who has
accountability for controls and delivery responsibilities

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:22.990
continues to have those. It is absolutely
essential - and I know they are doing this

00:16:22.990 --> 00:16:28.519
- that every National Director makes sure
that as they make their changes they are still

00:16:28.519 --> 00:16:35.519
capable of executing their accountabilities
and responsibilities; we would want to be

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:41.260
assured that they still have those arrangements
in place. There is some further assurance

00:16:41.260 --> 00:16:47.829
that we are going to be doing, as a team,
to ensure those controls which require working

00:16:47.829 --> 00:16:54.829
across directorates. In particular, where
National Directors require work to be done

00:16:55.630 --> 00:17:02.630
in the field we need to be assured that we
can still deliver on those accountabilities

00:17:05.260 --> 00:17:09.539
despite resources being reduced across the
organisation.

00:17:09.539 --> 00:17:15.449
I am not saying that there is not a risk there,
because there clearly is as we take resources

00:17:15.449 --> 00:17:22.449
out and make these changes happen. We are
very conscious of them and we will be ensuring

00:17:22.779 --> 00:17:28.529
that by putting all of this under a single
programme board, we will be managing those

00:17:28.529 --> 00:17:33.239
risks and watching those risks very carefully
as we go through the rest of the implementation.

00:17:33.239 --> 00:17:38.799
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Karen. Just to invite
Barbara to say a few words about, so far as

00:17:38.799 --> 00:17:43.929
the Field Force is concerned, if we are withdrawing
from activity, how that results in a realignment

00:17:43.929 --> 00:17:46.710
of responsibilities with CCGs and other players.

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:51.080
Barbara Hakin, National Director: Commissioning
Operations, NHS England: Thank you, Chairman.

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:57.389
If I can say first, I do know colleagues out
there are not terribly keen on the term 'Field

00:17:57.389 --> 00:18:03.359
Force'. I understand that, but what is absolutely
vital is that we have a way of describing

00:18:03.359 --> 00:18:13.019
you because you are such an incredibly essential
component of what we do. Apologies if it is

00:18:13.059 --> 00:18:16.919
a term you do not like, but it is a term which
has meaning to us and therefore I will carry

00:18:16.919 --> 00:18:19.970
on using it until we get a better alternative.

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:25.159
Ed, I am sure that those colleagues in the
Field Force will welcome what you said about

00:18:25.159 --> 00:18:29.929
the organisation needing to be clear what
its focus is, needing to be clear on some

00:18:29.929 --> 00:18:36.129
of the things that they can stop doing or
can be less of a priority, because it is very

00:18:36.129 --> 00:18:43.129
hard. There were small numbers in the first
instance spread across the geography. The

00:18:44.159 --> 00:18:49.739
re-organisation meant that whereas, once upon
a time, individuals were all together, they

00:18:49.739 --> 00:18:55.539
are now in several organisations: individuals
who worked together in a geography are now

00:18:55.539 --> 00:19:00.909
working in NHS England, Public Health England,
TDA. There were small numbers in the beginning,

00:19:00.909 --> 00:19:06.830
and this reduction will add to the strain
that they are feeling, and we as an organisation

00:19:06.830 --> 00:19:10.940
and as a leadership team, need to be very
clear about their priorities and where they

00:19:10.940 --> 00:19:17.940
need to put their time.

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:24.940
We have tried to make sure that by creating
a single tier - we had two tiers theoretically:

00:19:25.239 --> 00:19:32.239
the Regional teams and the Area teams - what
we were taking out was some of the waste.

00:19:36.489 --> 00:19:42.299
I am sure that everybody out there was working
really hard, but there was quite a bit of

00:19:42.299 --> 00:19:46.850
duplication; hopefully that efficiency will
help us and we will not just be putting a

00:19:46.850 --> 00:19:50.580
strain on fewer people doing the same amount
of work.

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:56.799
The other thing, I think, that is important
to remember is the changing nature of CCGs.

00:19:56.799 --> 00:20:02.960
When the organisation was set up CCGs were
very immature organisations. There were great

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:07.519
concerns about whether they would be able
to step up to the plate, and to some extent

00:20:07.519 --> 00:20:13.409
there was a feeling that our area teams were
the recreation of PCTs, whereas in reality,

00:20:13.409 --> 00:20:20.320
the CCGs are the recreation of the PCTs. With
the maturity of the CCGs, coupled with the

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:25.080
changing nature of our direction of travel,
our primary care co-commissioning, other aspects

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:31.779
of co-commissioning, it is really important
that our Field Force steps back and ensures

00:20:31.779 --> 00:20:33.139
that it does its major role.

00:20:33.139 --> 00:20:37.369
We have still got a significant role in direct
commission, although more of that is moving

00:20:37.369 --> 00:20:44.159
to CCGs, but our role in supporting and assuring
CCGs is a greater one, not doing for them

00:20:44.159 --> 00:20:50.519
what they now in many places have so ably
identified that they can do for themselves.

00:20:50.519 --> 00:20:55.960
That will include the representation of the
key Boards that Karen was talking about, such

00:20:55.960 --> 00:21:00.139
as the Health and Wellbeing Boards and the
Safeguarding Boards. NHS England has a statutory

00:21:00.139 --> 00:21:07.139
responsibility for Safeguarding and therefore
we will still need to ensure that we have

00:21:07.489 --> 00:21:11.970
the right level of involvement in Health and
Wellbeing boards.

00:21:11.970 --> 00:21:18.739
What we need to do is to ensure that, where
appropriate, CCGs can take on more responsibility.

00:21:18.739 --> 00:21:25.739
We need to start to be discerning about where
we would put our efforts for those CCGs who

00:21:28.419 --> 00:21:33.840
we feel are absolutely confident to represent
on Health and Wellbeing Boards - as I say,

00:21:33.840 --> 00:21:38.600
we have a statutory responsibility for Safeguarding
Boards which is different - but where we will

00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:44.600
put our efforts in supporting CCGs and where
we will need to have a greater intervention.

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:48.529
I think, if the Field Force were here, they
would say that in terms of the overarching

00:21:48.529 --> 00:21:53.129
CCG assurance processes it would be absolutely
critical that they can be discerning as to

00:21:53.129 --> 00:21:58.590
which CCGs they can give a lot of support
to and which CCGs have shown themselves to

00:21:58.590 --> 00:22:04.609
be very capable of being the local voice of
their communities, remembering of course that

00:22:04.609 --> 00:22:08.889
they are the ones who have local meetings
in public, therefore the more decisions that

00:22:08.889 --> 00:22:15.090
they are made by the CCGs, the more there
is local accountability to the population.

00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:21.350
I do not want to underestimate that there
are significant risks in reducing our staff

00:22:21.350 --> 00:22:25.429
numbers by this number, but we have a lot
of mitigations and with the way it has been

00:22:25.429 --> 00:22:30.690
done in the Field Force and the changing approach
to how to do business, hopefully it will be

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:35.129
able to allow us to deliver all that we want
to do.

00:22:35.129 --> 00:22:42.129
Margaret Casely-Hayford: It is absolutely
right that the CCGs should take on the responsibilities

00:22:42.629 --> 00:22:49.629
as described by Barbara. However, in my mind
I see a completely different field of operation

00:22:52.789 --> 00:22:58.710
residing with the area teams in that they
are the assurance side of things. It is not

00:22:58.710 --> 00:23:04.919
that they are supporting or duplicating what
the CCGs are doing. The CCGs are carrying

00:23:04.919 --> 00:23:11.919
out an executive function and the area teams
have an oversight and assurance on our behalf,

00:23:13.879 --> 00:23:20.879
really. Looking at the Forward Plan, the Individual
Care Programme suggests quite a lot of activity

00:23:24.389 --> 00:23:31.389
at ground-level. If you take that particular
programme, for example, the potential for

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:40.549
fraudulent networks springing up and undermining
the integrity of what one is trying to establish

00:23:40.549 --> 00:23:47.549
there is a possibility. I would just like
some sort of comfort, please, that we have

00:23:48.859 --> 00:23:55.859
the right number and the right level of support
to ensure the assurance and oversight of what

00:23:57.879 --> 00:24:04.809
are doing to make sure that the Forward Plan
works as it should. Forgive me if that sounds

00:24:04.809 --> 00:24:07.009
like a little too much detail.

00:24:07.009 --> 00:24:11.940
Barbara Hakin: No, not at all. I think that
is a really valid question. The Field Force,

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:17.429
if I can call it that, which currently is
made of all the regional teams and area teams

00:24:17.429 --> 00:24:24.429
but in the future will be a single integrated
tier which provides oversight and assurance,

00:24:24.599 --> 00:24:30.109
and some would say that the two tiers has
been too many layers of assurance and oversight.

00:24:30.109 --> 00:24:37.109
However, that Field Force will have a huge
range of things that it needs to deliver supported

00:24:37.289 --> 00:24:44.289
by the National Support Centre on behalf of
NHS England. Assurance will be its major role.

00:24:45.570 --> 00:24:50.179
In terms of many of the things we want to
see happen - the relationships with patients

00:24:50.179 --> 00:24:56.499
and the public, quality of care, experience
of care, all the things we were talking about

00:24:56.499 --> 00:25:02.379
in the planning round - we will be relying
on CCGs and their relationships with their

00:25:02.379 --> 00:25:09.169
local communities to deliver for us. NHS England
needs to be sure that this is happening, rather

00:25:09.169 --> 00:25:12.789
than necessarily stepping in and trying to
do it as an alternative. However, we will

00:25:12.789 --> 00:25:18.619
also still have a direct commissioning function,
so as well as that assurance of CCGs, the

00:25:18.619 --> 00:25:22.029
Field Force will still need to make sure that
in its direct commissioning function it is

00:25:22.029 --> 00:25:26.330
doing all the things you have already talked
about.

00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:33.080
The role is changing in terms of emphasis
and in terms of scale as CCGs mature, but

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:39.279
it is not fundamentally changing in what we
do. We have responsibility for making sure

00:25:39.279 --> 00:25:45.080
that the whole of the commissioning system
- whether it is our direct commissioning,

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:52.080
the oversight of CCGs, the way the CSUs support
our direct commissioners and CCGs - can be

00:25:55.159 --> 00:25:58.269
assured and assure you that it is doing everything
that it needs to.

00:25:58.269 --> 00:26:05.269
Ian Dodge: One specific additional observation
is that as well as providing assurance around

00:26:05.789 --> 00:26:09.970
the system, just connecting this conversation
to the earlier one on the Forward View and

00:26:09.970 --> 00:26:16.330
Ciaran's comments in particular around capacity
and capability for change, there will continue

00:26:16.330 --> 00:26:22.849
to be a very important role for the Field
Force in the NHS, working with our partners

00:26:22.849 --> 00:26:27.749
and their Field Forces and organisations like
the Academic Health Science Networks, Strategic

00:26:27.749 --> 00:26:34.139
Clinical Networks and Senates to think about
how they can collectively catalyse local system

00:26:34.139 --> 00:26:40.419
improvement. I just wanted to emphasise that
that transformational role is really critical

00:26:40.419 --> 00:26:47.330
alongside the assurance function.

00:26:47.330 --> 00:26:54.330
Ed Smith: There is, in my experience, a tendency
when organisations go through this for people

00:26:56.779 --> 00:27:03.779
to go into the shell of, 'This is now my job,
and I am not going to look outside my shell.'

00:27:04.739 --> 00:27:10.809
Actually, the reflex of leadership has to
be to create exactly the opposite set of behaviours,

00:27:10.809 --> 00:27:17.809
so the porosity of boundaries starts to increase
so that people go, 'How can I help? Who can

00:27:18.629 --> 00:27:24.279
I help? How do we do this together?' If I
can just suggest that as we go through this

00:27:24.279 --> 00:27:33.799
process, it starts in this room and it starts
with our teams. If we see people going into,

00:27:34.039 --> 00:27:41.659
'This is my job and it is not my job to do
anything else other than this,' that we push

00:27:41.729 --> 00:27:47.279
against that and we try to engage the spirit
of collective collaboration across all of

00:27:47.279 --> 00:27:51.799
your National Directorates and all of the
Field Force and other parts of the organisation.

00:27:51.799 --> 00:27:55.399
There is a natural tendency, I think, to go
the other way.

00:27:55.399 --> 00:27:59.649
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Ed. I think this
has been an immensely valuable discussion.

00:27:59.649 --> 00:28:06.649
I think the Board identifies and understands
the extent of risk that is inherent in this

00:28:07.190 --> 00:28:12.479
exercise, but I feel that the comments that
the Board has made in the responses that you

00:28:12.479 --> 00:28:17.690
have given us have given us a sufficient level
of assurance. However, it is something that

00:28:17.690 --> 00:28:23.749
we will want to watch very closely over the
coming phases of it, so thank you, Karen,

00:28:23.749 --> 00:28:25.809
for the paper.

00:28:25.809 --> 00:28:32.809
We now move to item eight on the agenda, which
is simply for noting the Board Committee feedback,

00:28:33.809 --> 00:28:40.809
and then item nine, which is any other business:
the date of the next meeting, which will be

00:28:41.719 --> 00:28:45.799
in Leeds on the 6th November. I have not been
notified of any other business that any member

00:28:45.799 --> 00:28:50.979
of the Board would wish to raise, and since
that is the case, I have only other one other

00:28:50.979 --> 00:28:54.679
request. I had only one other but I have a
further one. Tim.

00:28:54.679 --> 00:28:59.169
Tim Kelsey: Just one very small thing, but
people may be interested to go onto the NHS

00:28:59.169 --> 00:29:04.460
England website where they can find a version
of the annual report that I hope gives a better

00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:08.099
narrative of some of the impact NHS England
has had over the last year. That is called

00:29:08.099 --> 00:29:11.779
the Annual Review and it can be downloaded
from our website.

00:29:11.779 --> 00:29:16.889
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much. We will
take that home. My only other request to you

00:29:16.889 --> 00:29:21.649
was the formal one that we now move into a
meeting for confidential business. Thank you

00:29:21.649 --> 00:29:23.840
very much: the meeting is closed.

00:29:23.840 --> 00:29:26.760
Thank you very much, everybody, for attending
this morning.

